## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `ProductCatalogTests.cs`

**Summary: 10 tests, 10 findings — 6 Critical, 1 Medium, 1 Low**

This is a textbook **coverage-touching** test suite. Every public method on `ProductCatalog` is called, giving ~95% line coverage, but **7 of 10 tests have zero meaningful assertions**. These tests cannot catch regressions — they only prove the code doesn't throw.

---

### 🔴 CRITICAL — Tests giving false confidence

#### 1. Coverage-Touching Pattern (systemic)

The entire suite methodically calls every public method in declaration order (`Add`, `Get`, `UpdatePrice`, `Remove`, `Search`, `GetAll`, `GetProductCount`, `ApplyBulkDiscount`, `ExportToCsv`, `ImportFromCsv`) with no real verification. This is the root cause of your "95% coverage, bugs in production" problem.

---

#### 2. No Assertions — 4 tests assert literally nothing

| Line | Method | Problem |
|------|--------|---------|
| 17–21 | `AddProduct_Works` | Calls `AddProduct`, never checks the product was stored |
| 33–36 | `UpdatePrice_Works` | Calls `UpdatePrice`, never checks the new price |
| 39–43 | `RemoveProduct_Works` | Calls `RemoveProduct`, never checks deletion |
| 63–66 | `GetProductCount_Works` | Gets count, never asserts the value |
| 68–74 | `ApplyBulkDiscount_Works` | Applies 10% discount, never checks prices changed |
| 84–88 | `ImportFromCsv_Works` | Imports CSV, never checks products exist |

**Fix for `AddProduct_Works` (line 17):**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void AddProduct_Works()
{
    var product = new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m);
    _catalog.AddProduct(product);

    var stored = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.AreEqual("Widget", stored?.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, stored?.Price);
}
```

